WA-2025-SB6034.summary:
Codifying Governor's Office of Indian Affairs. The GOIA is established as a cabinet agency of the Governor. The executive director of GOIA is appointed by, and serves at the pleasure of the Governor as part of the Governor's executive cabinet. The Governor's appointee for executive director of GOIA is subject to Senate confirmation. The responsibilities of GOIA are as follows:
• advise state agencies and departments concerning issues relative to federally recognized Indian tribes;
• be the Governor's liaison between the state of Washington, Indian tribal governments, and Indian organizations;
• be a liaison and advisor to the Governor and state agencies on legislation and policies in Indian affairs;
• provide assistance and advice to state agencies, departments, boards and commissions concerning tribal relationships and issues impacting tribal rights, communities, lands, and interests;
• regularly convene meetings with tribal nations and relevant state parties to discuss issues of mutual interest; and
• facilitate and convene meetings with tribal leaders annually to implement the Centennial Accords.

GOIA is authorized to establish procedures and requirements for operations and expenditures to support and enhance government partnership and relationships with tribal nations. GOIA may accept or request gifts or grants from citizens and other private sources.

Training. GOIA must provide a training focused on effective communication and collaboration between the state and federally recognized Indian tribes, which must be attended by the heads of each executive and small cabinet agencies, and each of the Governor's policy advisors and executive leadership team. In partnership with other agencies, GOIA must expand upon the statute governing government-to-government relationships to produce additional training for state employees.

Reporting Requirements. On or before November 1st of each year, GOIA must submit to the Governor and the Legislature an annual written report of the operations, activities, programs, and services of the office for the preceding fiscal year. The report must include several components, including but not limited to a description of the operation, activity, program, or service; budget data; goals and challenges; and relevant statutory references and requirements.

WA-2025-HB2211.summary:
"Medically tailored meals" are defined as fresh or frozen meals that are designed by a qualified medical professional for the treatment of a person's medical conditions. Qualified medical professionals include health care providers whose credential include the authority to establish and tailor nutrition care plans to meet a client's needs.

If the Department of Social and Health Services or the Health Care Authority contracts with entities to provide medically tailored meals to enrollees of medical assistance programs, the entities must be Washington State-based, nonprofit organizations, to the extent possible. Medically tailored meal vendors must comply with the client's nutrition care plan and have a qualified medical professional review and approve the menus provided in response to a nutrition care plan.

Medically tailored meals provided to enrollees of medical assistance programs must:
• align with evidence-based nutritional practice guidelines for treating the client's specific condition;
• offer accommodations in dietary needs, allergy restrictions, and cultural preferences;
• prioritize local, whole foods or from-scratch prepared meals;
• provide at least 500 calories or otherwise meet a client's medically appropriate energy needs; and
• provide one-third of the recommended dietary reference intakes for carbohydrates and protein, as established by the Food and Nutrition Board of the National Academy of Medicine.

WA-2025-HB2420.summary:
After December 31, 2026, the maximum cost of a public works project eligible for use of the small works roster is increased in phases as follows:
• $530,000 or less from January 1, 2027 to June 30, 2027;
• $560,000 or less from July 1, 2027 to June 30, 2028;
• $590,000 or less from July 1, 2028 to June 30, 2029;
• $620,000 or less from July 1, 2029 to June 30, 2030; and
• $650,000 or less beginning July 1, 2030.
